revise(2,[('It does not enforce local receptive fields or spatial weight sharing, although the values can be reshaped back.','Flattening is reversible when the original shape/order is retained; a generic dense layer does not encode convolutional locality and sharing.'),('It preserves convolutional weight sharing because adjacent pixels remain adjacent vector entries.','Vector order preserves indexing, not a constraint tying learned weights at different spatial positions.'),('It enforces local receptive fields because each pixel becomes a separate input feature.','A dense neuron can connect to every input; separate feature indices do not restrict receptive fields.'),('It removes positional order because a dense layer treats the flattened values as an unordered set.','Ordinary dense layers use indexed weights, so permuting inputs without permuting weights generally changes the output.')])
